TASK A3 – DISCOVER THE DATA-ACCESS DESIGN PATTERN 
Problem: A student project contains three classes: StudentDB, CourseDB and MarksDB. All three classes perform similar database operations, resulting in repeated code.

The solution is to introduce the Data Access Object (DAO) Design Pattern using a generic interface.
A3.1 – Identify the Common Pattern (2 Marks)
The three classes perform almost identical database operations.

Common steps repeated in all three classes:
1. Establish a database connection using JDBC.
2. Prepare an SQL query.
3. Execute the SQL statement.
4. Retrieve the results using ResultSet.
5. Convert database records into Java objects.
6. Close the database resources.
Parts that differ:
StudentDB	CourseDB	MarksDB
Student table	Course table	Marks table
Student ID	Course ID	Marks ID
Student name	Course name	Marks details
Student object	Course object	Marks object
Student-specific SQL	Course-specific SQL	Marks-specific SQL

Identified Design Pattern:

The repeated functionality can be separated using the Data Access Object (DAO) Pattern.

The DAO pattern separates database access logic from business logic. It provides a common structure for performing database operations and reduces code duplication.

Advantages:
- Reduces repeated code.
- Improves code reusability.
- Makes maintenance easier.
- Separates database operations from business logic.
- Makes testing easier.

A3.2 – Design a Generic Data-Access Interface (2 Marks)
A generic interface Dao<T, K> can be created to perform common database operations.

Here:
- T represents the entity type, such as Student or Course.
- K represents the primary key type, such as Integer or String.
1. Generic DAO Interface
Java Code:
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public interface Dao<T, K> {

    void insert(T entity);

    Optional<T> findById(K id);

    List<T> findAll();

    void update(T entity);

    void delete(K id);
}


Explanation of Methods:
Method	Purpose
insert(T entity)	Inserts a new record
findById(K id)	Finds a record using its primary key
findAll()	Retrieves all records
update(T entity)	Updates an existing record
delete(K id)	Deletes a record using its primary key

2. StudentDao Implementation
The StudentDao class implements the generic DAO interface for Student records.

Java Code:
import java.sql.*;
import java.util.*;

public class StudentDao implements Dao<Student, Integer> {

    private Connection connection;

    public StudentDao(Connection connection) {
        this.connection = connection;
    }

    @Override
    public void insert(Student student) {

        String sql =
            "INSERT INTO student VALUES (?, ?)";

        try (PreparedStatement ps =
                 connection.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            ps.setInt(1, student.getId());
            ps.setString(2, student.getName());

            ps.executeUpdate();

        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Student> findById(Integer id) {

        String sql =
            "SELECT * FROM student WHERE id = ?";

        try (PreparedStatement ps =
                 connection.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            ps.setInt(1, id);

            try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {

                if (rs.next()) {

                    Student student = new Student(
                        rs.getInt("id"),
                        rs.getString("name")
                    );

                    return Optional.of(student);
                }
            }

        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        return Optional.empty();
    }

    @Override
    public List<Student> findAll() {

        List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

        String sql = "SELECT * FROM student";

        try (
            Statement st = connection.createStatement();
            ResultSet rs = st.executeQuery(sql)
        ) {

            while (rs.next()) {

                students.add(new Student(
                    rs.getInt("id"),
                    rs.getString("name")
                ));
            }

        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        return students;
    }

    @Override
    public void update(Student student) {

        String sql =
            "UPDATE student SET name = ? WHERE id = ?";

        try (PreparedStatement ps =
                 connection.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            ps.setString(1, student.getName());
            ps.setInt(2, student.getId());

            ps.executeUpdate();

        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void delete(Integer id) {

        String sql =
            "DELETE FROM student WHERE id = ?";

        try (PreparedStatement ps =
                 connection.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            ps.setInt(1, id);
            ps.executeUpdate();

        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}


Note: The example assumes the Student class has a constructor accepting an integer ID and a String name. In a complete application, database exceptions should be propagated to the service layer rather than silently swallowed.
