A3.3 – Map Objects to Tables (1 Mark)
Object-relational mapping (ORM) is the process of mapping Java class fields to database table columns.

For the Student class, the mapping is:
Student Java Class
public class Student {

    private int id;
    private String name;

    public Student(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}


Student Database Table
Table name: student
Java class field	Database column	Data type	Constraint
id	id	INT	PRIMARY KEY
name	name	VARCHAR(100)	NOT NULL

SQL Table Creation:
CREATE TABLE student (
    id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL
);


Object-to-Table Mapping:
Student Object              Student Table

id = 101          ------->   id = 101
name = "Arun"     ------->   name = "Arun"


The id field acts as the primary key and uniquely identifies each student record.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Alternative: Writing SQL Directly Inside Service or GUI Classes
Instead of using a generic DAO interface, SQL queries can be written directly inside service classes or GUI classes.

Example:
public class StudentService {

    public void addStudent(
        Connection con, int id, String name
    ) {

        String sql =
            "INSERT INTO student VALUES (?, ?)";

        try (PreparedStatement ps =
                 con.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            ps.setInt(1, id);
            ps.setString(2, name);

            ps.executeUpdate();

        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}


In this approach, database connection handling, SQL queries and result processing are mixed with application logic.
Comparison of Both Approaches
Criteria	Generic DAO Pattern	SQL Inside Service/GUI
Code duplication	Low	High
Maintainability	Easy	Difficult
Reusability	High	Low
Separation of concerns	Clear	Poor
Testing	Easier	More difficult
Database changes	Centralized	Changes required in multiple places

Comparison When a Table Column Is Renamed
Suppose the student table column name is renamed to student_name.

Using the DAO pattern:
- The SQL query and result mapping inside StudentDao must be updated.
- Other service and GUI classes can continue using the same DAO methods.
- Changes are mainly restricted to the data-access implementation.
Using SQL directly inside service or GUI classes:
- Every SQL query referring to name must be located.
- Multiple service and GUI classes may need modifications.
- This increases maintenance effort and the possibility of errors.
Time and Space Complexity Analysis
Let \(n\) represent the number of records retrieved from the database.
Operation	Generic DAO	SQL inside Service/GUI
Insert	O(1) database operation	O(1) database operation
Find by primary key	O(1) with indexed primary key	O(1) with indexed primary key
Find all	O(n)	O(n)
Update	O(1) with indexed primary key	O(1) with indexed primary key
Delete	O(1) with indexed primary key	O(1) with indexed primary key

These are typical database-operation complexities assuming an indexed primary key. Actual performance depends on the database engine, indexing, query execution plan and transaction overhead.

Space Complexity:
- Generic DAO: O(n) when retrieving and storing all records in a list; O(1) auxiliary space for a single-record operation.
- SQL inside Service/GUI: O(n) when retrieving all records; O(1) auxiliary space for a single-record operation.
Important observation: Both approaches have approximately the same database time and space complexity when executing equivalent SQL queries. The DAO pattern does not automatically make SQL execution faster. Its main advantage is better code organization, reusability and maintainability.
Final Comparison and Decision
I would select the Generic DAO Design Pattern because it separates database operations from business logic and reduces code duplication.

If the database structure changes, the modifications can be handled mainly inside the relevant DAO implementation instead of changing multiple service and GUI classes.

Therefore, the DAO pattern is more suitable for large applications containing multiple database tables because it improves maintainability, scalability and code reusability.
